{"kind":"artifact","text":"The two refusal sentences live in one place, so the adapter and the command line cannot drift apart on what an untrustworthy record means. Exact clean pushed head 128132f23c3ea13e7b0ed93e33735f6181531c92; tree unchanged from 9d3e6483, checkout clean, origin matching. Under planner's child 467ad483 and my promise 5ee01d7b.\n\nWHAT IS HERE. UntrustedAdvertisement at line 84 and UnusableAdvertisedURL at line 90: the shared clause each surface wraps with its own way out. cmd/gs and the MCP adapter now build the same sentence from the same function, where before each held its own format string saying nearly the same thing.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND TIDINESS. These sentences are what a person sees when the record telling their tools where to send a signed act cannot be trusted. Two independently maintained wordings for one security condition is how one of them quietly stops saying the important part. Making it one function means a change to what the refusal admits has to be made once, deliberately, rather than in two places by whoever remembers both.\n\nWHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO. It does not decide what either caller does about the refusal. The adapter refuses a durable act and still answers a read locally; cmd/gs refuses and names --server - as the way to fold on purpose. Each surface keeps its own recovery because each has a different one; only the statement of the condition is shared.\n\nGATES, from runs I watched exit at this exact head. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0: 35 packages, 34 ok and 1 with no test files, zero failures and zero cached — cmd/gs 318.689s, internal/kernel 245.744s, internal/app 243.478s, cmd/gitseq-mcp 206.823s, internal/docset 172.454s. npm test 87 pass, 0 fail. gofmt -l . empty; go build and go vet exit 0; git diff --check exit 0; make ui leaves git status empty, so the embed is byte-reproduced; make ui-check exit 0. The first UI run in this worktree failed five files with \"Cannot find module react\" — that was 